5417] [17592186485469 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485469 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e9f3276f-779b-51ad-9d6b-890a9e89a2ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484909 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485198 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186485255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186485226 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485470 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ec35b09d-4f30-50dc-a52b-44c7b5d9523b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485505 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485505 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485505 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485505 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485505 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "203918fb-bff3-5640-9116-ee4c833a081b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485505 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The number of rows in $A$ is not the same as the number of columns. So, it's not a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\cdot A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not a square matrix and ${A}^{T}\\cdot A\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485512 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve for $x$ in $Ax=b$ given that the matrix $A$ is orthogonal, and \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "340e44da-12e6-5ccb-a96a-bc97b2d0d915" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485512 #edb/ref 1074 "The inverse of an orthogonal matrix is equal to its transpose.\n\nHence, since $A$ is orthogonal, we have ${A}^{-1}={A}^{T}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}Ax & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\ x & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ x & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485514 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485366 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485514 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84784" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485514 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485514 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nI. If $A=PB{P}^{-1}$ then $|det(A)|=|det(B)|$\n\nII. $PQ$ is always orthogonal\n\nIII. $2P+Q$ is **not** always orthogonal" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485514 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35c37699-f2ab-5493-8b62-dd508d98be99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485514 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing $|det(A)|,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|det(A)| & =|det(PB{P}^{-1})| \\\\  & =|det(PB{P}^{T})| \\\\  & =|det(P)|\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot |det({P}^{T})| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |det(B)|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|det(B)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the product $(PQ)\\cdot (PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PQ\\cdot (PQ)^{T} & =(PQ)\\cdot {Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =P{P}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. As a counterexample, consider the following sum involving two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n2[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, the first column is not normalized.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485516 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485371 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485516 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84245" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485516 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485516 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{18}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3} & \\sqrt{8} \\\\ \\sqrt{8} & 4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind $|\\text{det}(PQ)|$ given that $P$ is an orthogonal matrix." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485516 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "36bba55d-9122-58f3-aa1e-57a8674f5e02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485516 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the following facts: \n\n- The determinant of a product is equal to the product of the determinants.\n\n- The determinant of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is equal to $\\pm 1.$\n\nApplying these ideas, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\text{det}(PQ)| & =|\\text{det}(P)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|(\\pm 1)\\cdot \\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\pm 1|\\cdot |\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\text{det}(Q)| \\\\  & =|\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{8}\\cdot \\sqrt{8}| \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485524 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485524 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485524 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485524 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "46db878d-0e5d-50fb-979a-d499bbe2ee60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485524 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is false. Note that\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{24}{25} \\\\ \\frac{24}{25} & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A\\cdot {A}^{T}\\ne I,$ our matrix is **not** orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-140564" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485529 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the orthogonal matrix $Q?$\n\nI. ${Q}^{2}$ is orthogonal\n\nII. $Q$ is a diagonal matrix\n\nIII. $Q-I$ is invertible" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4cd1f663-1b26-5239-80df-41dfbaf9dd53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485529 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{2}\\cdot ({Q}^{2})^{T} & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot (Q\\cdot Q)^{T} \\\\  & =(Q\\cdot Q)\\cdot ({Q}^{T}\\cdot {Q}^{T}) \\\\  & =Q\\cdot (Q\\cdot {Q}^{T})\\cdot {Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q(I){Q}^{T} \\\\  & =Q{Q}^{T} \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. As a counterexample, consider the matrix:\n\n$$\nQ=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n$Q$ is an orthogonal matrix, but it is not a diagonal matrix.\n\n- Statement III is false. As a counterexample, let $Q=I$ be the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix. Then, \n\n$$\nQ-I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nthe $2\\times 2$  zero matrix, which is not an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485459 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485551 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485551 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "83aaff63-f033-5323-bd52-af10976e5a16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485551 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{5} & \\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485577 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186485410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485577 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485577 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485577 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is a square matrix\n\nII. $A\\cdot {A}^{T}=I$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485577 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bb8ec734-9246-5393-99e6-34bea8046fc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186485577 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that a square matrix $A$ is **orthogonal** if and only if\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}={A}^{T}\\cdot A=I\n$$\n\nwhere $I$ is the identity matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given statements. \n\n- Statement I is true. The number of rows in $A$ is the same as the number of columns. So, it's a square matrix.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\nA\\cdot {A}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 & -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=I.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${A}^{T}\\cdot A=I,$ our matrix is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
